  <h2 id="what">What it is</h2>
  <p><code>paros-consensus</code> holds the Paxos roles as plain state machines: proposer,
  acceptor, replica, matchmaker, proxy leader, batcher, leaderless reader. It does no I/O, it
  reads no clock and it draws no random number. It uses std only, and with
  <code>default-features = false</code> it has no dependency. Your program, the driver, owns the
  sockets, the disk and the timers. So the same code runs in a unit test, in a simulation, in this
  browser (compiled to wasm) and behind a real network.</p>
  <p>It is a general library. You can use it for plain Multi-Paxos with a fixed membership, or for
  one single-decree choice, with no part of the paros journal. A deployment runs what its
  <code>Config</code> names: the quorum system, the matchmakers, the proxy leaders and the replicas.
  No cargo feature changes a decision. With no matchmakers, plain Multi-Paxos sends no matchmaker
  message and writes no extra state, and a reconfiguration is refused. What the paros journal needs
  comes from optional behaviour and composition, never from a service concept inside the core
  (<a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/architecture/04-roles.md#43-the-consensus-core-is-a-library-for-everyone`}>architecture §4.3</a>).</p>

  <h2 id="flavors">Each flavor, and where it is</h2>
  <p>Each row is a flavor that a <a href={url("paxos/")}>Paxos page</a> teaches, the type that
  builds it, and an example that runs it. Run an example with
  <code>cargo run -p paros-consensus --example &lt;name&gt;</code>: each prints a trace and asserts
  the property that it shows.</p>
    <table>
